                        "form": "die Kausalitäten"
                    }
                ],
                "senses": [
                    {
                        "sense_id": "f50d07ee-1a19-3a80-9d4a-8d2277867391",
                        "entry_id": "d42c189f-ac56-36c0-b11d-d72d9ae55148",
                        "glosses": [
                            "Ursache und Wirkung im Zusammenhang; Prinzip, nach dem jede Wirkung eine Ursache hat oder Ursache einer anderen Wirkung ist"
                        ],
                        "tags": [
                            "physics"
                        ],
                        "examples": [
                            {
                                "example_id": "954274c5-8230-4a56-ad6b-c421158d26c0",
                                "sense_id": "f50d07ee-1a19-3a80-9d4a-8d2277867391",
                                "text": "„Einstein forderte zur Aufrechterhaltung der Kausalität, daß es keine Signale mit Überlichtgeschwindigkeit geben darf (Kausalitätsprinzip).“"
                            }
                        ],
                        "sense_index_json": "\"1\""
                    },
                    {
                        "sense_id": "d4d1c077-d192-3e1c-86f7-6015113fbf17",
                        "entry_id": "d42c189f-ac56-36c0-b11d-d72d9ae55148",
                        "glosses": [
                            "Verbindung zwischen Handlung des Täters und dem Erfolgseintritt"
                        ],
                        "tags": [
                            "Strafrecht"
                        ],
                        "examples": [
                            {
                                "example_id": "076b5605-7a68-4f5a-807e-6a5dad7d6baa",
                                "sense_id": "d4d1c077-d192-3e1c-86f7-6015113fbf17",
                                "text": "Im Allgemeinen wird die Kausalität im Strafrecht nach der Conditio-sine-qua-non-Formel bestimmt."
                            }
                        ],
                        "sense_index_json": "\"2\""
                    }
                ],
                "translations": [
                    {
                        "translation_id": "d35ea32b-34e5-44e9-bae5-508a332ad6c8",
                        "source_entry_id": "d42c189f-ac56-36c0-b11d-d72d9ae55148",
                        "source_sense_id": "f50d07ee-1a19-3a80-9d4a-8d2277867391",
                        "source_sense_description": "Ursache und Wirkung im Zusammenhang",
                        "target_lang_word": "causality",
                        "target_lang_code": "en",
                        "target_sense_ids": [],
                        "cross_references": [
                            {
                                "target_word": "causality",
                                "target_lang_code": "en",
                                "target_senses": [
                                    {
                                        "sense_id": "91540739-64a6-30d8-ab2d-1ab68d9ddda5",
                                        "entry_id": "9f9615f5-d662-3c3e-af4f-01a09629ac83",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "The agency of a cause; the action or power of a cause, in producing its effect."
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [
                                            "countable",
                                            "uncountable"
                                        ],
                                        "examples": []
                                    },
                                    {
                                        "sense_id": "6344ac5a-b15f-33cf-b7b6-5994456acf43",
                                        "entry_id": "9f9615f5-d662-3c3e-af4f-01a09629ac83",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "The relationship between something that happens or exists and the thing that causes it; the cause and consequence relationship."
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [
                                            "countable",
                                            "uncountable"
                                        ],
                                        "examples": [
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "11f6fd0d-0ca1-41ef-bda1-b3ef0ae697ab",
                                                "sense_id": "6344ac5a-b15f-33cf-b7b6-5994456acf43",
                                                "text": "But how do transformations like the evolution of language take place? A scientist looks for a cause inside time; a mystic knows that causality is essentially a process that is outside time-space."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "148a090e-ea52-4a12-99f3-362a2cec45f8",
                                                "sense_id": "6344ac5a-b15f-33cf-b7b6-5994456acf43",
                                                "text": "But some discussion of the complex relationship between “allohistory” and sf is appropriate here, as the genres overlap in certain ways. Classical allohistory— such as Trevelyan's \"What if Napoleon had won the Battle of Waterloo?\" and Churchill's \"If Lee had not won the Battle of Gettysburg\" —is a rigorously consistent thought-experiment in historical causality."
                                            }
                                        ]
                                    }
                                ]
                            }
                        ]
                    },
                    {
                        "translation_id": "aca02fe9-facf-4bd0-916c-fb1e647c5f36",
                        "source_entry_id": "d42c189f-ac56-36c0-b11d-d72d9ae55148",
                        "source_sense_id": "f50d07ee-1a19-3a80-9d4a-8d2277867391",
                        "source_sense_description": "Ursache und Wirkung im Zusammenhang",
                        "target_lang_word": "causalité",
                        "target_lang_code": "fr",
                        "target_sense_ids": [],
                        "cross_references": [
                            {
                                "target_word": "causalité",
                                "target_lang_code": "fr",
                                "target_senses": [
                                    {
                                        "sense_id": "82b88827-7808-345b-8e86-61c6f642cb02",
                                        "entry_id": "55c488a3-ceae-3bdc-93fb-b6f999106684",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "Rapport de cause à effet."
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [
                                            "Didactique"
                                        ],
                                        "examples": [
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "eaac5631-b6bd-4956-9259-401a02d0a4b0",
                                                "sense_id": "82b88827-7808-345b-8e86-61c6f642cb02",
                                                "text": "Il y a entre ces deux choses un rapport de causalité."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "47c22533-9a6f-43fd-a66c-693a020a344f",
                                                "sense_id": "82b88827-7808-345b-8e86-61c6f642cb02",
                                                "text": "Ce qui distingue précisément toute science d’un simple agrégat, c’est que chaque connaissance y dérive d’une connaissance antérieure, comme de son principe. Aussi Platon dit-il : « Même les opinions vraies sont de peu de valeur, tant qu’on ne les a pas enchaînées par un raisonnement de causalité. » (Platon, Ménon)"
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "0672a5c2-8ad1-49cd-869f-71e9a4802420",
                                                "sense_id": "82b88827-7808-345b-8e86-61c6f642cb02",
                                                "text": "Occam ne nie pas le principe de causalité et sa valeur ; mais il nie que nous puissions connaître en un être une puissance active, c’est-à-dire quelque chose qui, en lui, se réfère à un être autre que lui ; la connaissance d’un être se termine à lui-même."
                                            }
                                        ]
                                    }
                                ]
                            }
                        ]
                    },
                    {
                        "translation_id": "63911772-3d04-4675-bf58-efc391978bb7",
                        "source_entry_id": "d42c189f-ac56-36c0-b11d-d72d9ae55148",
